<h2 class="section-title">ABSTRACT</h2>

<p>Inclusive education remains one of the most critical societal objectives recognized by the United Nations Sustainable Development Goal 4 (SDG 4). However, visually impaired students continue to encounter severe systemic impediments in secondary and tertiary education, particularly within computer-based testing, assessments, and independent portal navigation. Conventional academic testing environments overwhelmingly mandate the physical presence of human scribes. Human scribes introduce significant operational bottlenecks, subjective interpretive bias, privacy compromises, scheduling conflicts, and high financial overheads.</p>

<p>To resolve these longstanding barriers, this project presents <strong>VisionAssist AI</strong>, an end-to-end, autonomous, voice-driven examination and academic management ecosystem engineered specifically for visually impaired scholars, educators, and administrators.</p>

<p>The system incorporates three core technological innovations:<br>1. <strong>Autonomous Hands-Free Audio Navigation Engine:</strong> Eliminates the cognitive burden of screen readers by deploying a full-viewport tactile trigger ("Tap Anywhere to Speak") coupled with continuous real-time Natural Language Speech Recognition (STT) and multi-accented Neural Text-to-Speech (TTS) synthesis.<br>2. <strong>Accessible Computer-Based Examination Pipeline:</strong> Facilitates question paper delivery through audio narration, real-time verbal dictation of long-form and objective answers, instantaneous transcript confirmation, and automated submission workflows.<br>3. <strong>Real-Time Computer Vision Proctoring with Multi-Person Intruder Detection:</strong> An automated, client-side proctoring engine executing on the live video stream that registers the authenticated student's facial profile, calculates continuous face presence tolerance (5-second threshold), and rigorously tracks multiple-person presence. If an unverified person enters the camera field or the student leaves the frame, the proctor enforces a strict <strong>2-warning audio-visual alert protocol before terminating the examination on the 3rd violation</strong> to preserve absolute academic integrity without requiring human proctors.</p>

<p>The platform is designed around a three-tier decoupled architecture comprising a modern React 18 client optimized with Web Accessibility Initiative Accessible Rich Internet Applications (WAI-ARIA) specifications, a Node.js/Express RESTful micro-service layer, and a cloud MongoDB Atlas database. Comprehensive empirical evaluations across simulated testing scenarios demonstrated an average Speech Recognition Accuracy of 96.4%, face presence detection latency of under 45ms per frame, and 100% detection rate for unauthorized multi-person intrusions. The platform successfully bridges the educational accessibility divide, empowering visually impaired students to take certified examinations with independence, confidentiality, and integrity.</p>

<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> Assistive Educational Technology, Web Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA), Speech-to-Text (STT), Text-to-Speech (TTS), AI Proctoring, Computer Vision, Facial Recognition, Multi-Person Intrusion Detection.</p>

