<h1 class="chapter-title">CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE SURVEY & RELATED WORK</h1>

<h3 class="subsection-title">2.1 Assistive Technologies in Modern Education</h3>
Assistive technologies for individuals with visual disabilities have progressed through three distinct epochs:
1. <strong>Mechanical Era (1950–1980):</strong> Dominated by mechanical Braille writers (Perkins Brailler) and analog cassette tape recordings of books.
2. <strong>Digital Screen Reader Era (1990–2015):</strong> Characterized by software applications parsing operating system accessibility trees (e.g., JAWS, Window-Eyes, and NVDA).
3. <strong>Cognitive AI Era (2016–Present):</strong> Marked by natural language conversational agents, on-device neural computer vision, and context-aware speech synthesis.

<h3 class="subsection-title">2.2 Critical Analysis of Existing Screen Readers</h3>
While generic screen readers like NVDA (NonVisual Desktop Access) and JAWS (Job Access With Speech) are indispensable tools for general operating system use, they present severe drawbacks when used by students taking timed examinations:
- <strong>Excessive Cognitive Load:</strong> Screen readers read every DOM element, including headers, link paths, advertisements, and layout divs. The student must master dozens of arcane keyboard shortcuts (e.g., `Insert + Down Arrow`, `H` for headings, `Tab` for focus) to locate a question.
- <strong>Desynchronization during Live Examination:</strong> Complex client-side dynamic DOM updates often fail to trigger ARIA live updates, causing the examinee to miss crucial timer alerts or submission dialogs.
- <strong>Lack of Proctoring Integration:</strong> Generic screen readers provide zero awareness of examination integrity and can easily conflict with lockdown browsers or video proctoring plug-ins.

<h3 class="subsection-title">2.3 Scribe-Assisted Examinations vs. Autonomous Testing</h3>
Numerous academic studies (e.g., Blenkhorn et al., 2021; UNESCO Accessibility Guidelines, 2022) have documented the disadvantages of physical human scribes:
- <strong>The Scribe Competence Paradox:</strong> An examinee who understands advanced mathematical algorithms or programming syntax cannot convey their solution effectively if the assigned scribe lacks computer science knowledge.
- <strong>Psychological Pressure:</strong> Sighted examinees can write and erase answers freely. Visually impaired examinees using scribes report feeling embarrassed when asking a scribe to rewrite sentences multiple times.

<h3 class="subsection-title">2.4 Survey of Computer Vision in Automated Proctoring</h3>
Automated proctoring has emerged as a major field of research. Classical approaches rely on server-side streaming where raw video frames are transmitted to cloud GPU clusters. However, this model suffers from high bandwidth consumption and privacy risks. Modern paradigms prioritize <strong>edge computing / client-side WebAssembly computer vision</strong>. Using browser-based inference models (such as MediaPipe and TensorFlow.js), face detection, head pose estimation, and gaze tracking can be executed locally on the user's CPU/GPU without transmitting raw video over the network.

<h3 class="subsection-title">2.5 Comparative Analysis Matrix</h3>

